"""Build offline learner resources from editable source; no audio or course edits."""
from pathlib import Path
import html,json,sys
R=Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve().parent
course=json.loads(Path(sys.argv[1]).read_text())
items=''.join('<dt>'+html.escape(g['term'])+'</dt><dd>'+html.escape(g['tooltip'])+'</dd>' for g in course['term_glossary'])
(R/"glossary.html").write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>FCSA glossary</title><style>body{font:18px/1.65 system-ui;max-width:850px;margin:auto;padding:32px;color:#5f6062}h1{color:#343895}dt{font-weight:700;margin-top:25px;color:#4e682b}dd{margin:6px 0}</style><h1>FCSA course glossary</h1><dl>'+items+'</dl></html>')
(R/'resources').mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
spec='https://www.opencompute.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/FCSA-1.1.0.pdf'
ex=[
(1,'Bound the claim',6,'A package contains application processors and an accelerator sharing one OS. Another package contains a server processor and an independently managed DPU, each supporting its own system software. A supplier calls both packages “FCSA compatible.”',
['Draw or describe the system software boundary in each package.','Rewrite the supplier claim with the version, type, level, and evidence still to be supplied.','Identify one design or profile agreement that a foundation claim alone would not settle.'],
'The first description is a candidate partitioned system. The second can contain two separate systems; package co-location does not merge them. A useful claim identifies FCSA 1.1.0, the scoped system/chiplet types, applicable level and rules, and the evidence. Product requirements and design/profile agreements remain explicit. The scenarios do not provide enough evidence to conclude compliance.',[15,16,18,20,24]),
(2,'Classify with evidence',7,'Candidate A has caches participating in system coherency and reports a remote translation arrangement. Candidate B has no coherent cache, makes coherent accesses, and sends untranslated requests to a host-side translation/protection function. Both are described as accelerators.',
['State which architectural facts differ and which type sections you would inspect.','List the mandatory-interface evidence and configuration assumptions still missing.','Explain why neither supplier’s process node would settle the type.'],
'A points toward Fully Coherent Expansion (Remote Translation); B points toward I/O Coherent Expansion (Untranslated). These are candidate classifications, not established compliance. Verify the exact functionality, translation/protection boundary, mandatory interfaces, system-side responsibilities, intended configuration, and applicable rules. Multiple classifications remain possible if the implementation meets each type’s requirements.',[18,19,56,62,72,76]),
(3,'Find the boot dependency',7,'A proposed subordinate receives its second-stage image only through an operational memory traffic interface. That interface is initialized by the image being loaded. The proposal also labels one block “management” without separating sequence control, system control policy, and security.',
['Describe the circular dependency and the activity-state evidence required.','Compare distributed, shared, and independent image-loading alternatives without selecting an unsupported protocol.','Assign the image supplier, synchronization authority, resource-control owner, and firmware-authentication responsibility.'],
'The image cannot be required to initialize its only delivery path. Establish a path available at the needed boot state, checking the pair’s rules and conditional implementation alternatives. Distributed means the manager loads the subordinate image; shared means each fetches from one shared NVM; independent uses separate NVMs. Ready/Proceed/Error coordinates progress but does not establish authenticity. Assign each responsibility and keep TO BE DEFINED mappings visible.',[91,92,131,132,133,134,139,148,151]),
(4,'Reopen a trust decision',8,'An accelerator supplied accepted identity/state evidence at boot. A service action later unlocks debug, changing a security-relevant condition. An engineer proposes logging the event while retaining the earlier trust decision and lowering residual impact because the change was detected.',
['Name the assumption and evidence invalidated by the change.','Specify the appraisal authority, fresh evidence, and product-defined response needed before continued reliance.','Choose a CAST treatment and identify the evidence needed to justify residual risk.'],
'The earlier evidence describes an earlier state. CRoT-A retains local responsibilities and reports relevant state; System RoT appraises fresh evidence against authorized composition and policy. Revalidation and a defined, validated response are needed. Detection alone does not justify lower impact. A product might restrict operation, isolate, recover, or take another justified action; no universal outcome is supplied by this example. CAST means Control, Accept, Suppress, Transfer.',[165,166,167,168,175,176]),
(5,'Recommend the next action',8,'The accelerator changes its boot arrangement. The system adds coherent requester chiplets. A robotics variant introduces a control loop and package-wide fault-response expectations. Available evidence covers only the previous two-requester configuration.',
['Assign an owner and artifact for each changed assumption.','Separate published FCSA requirements, implementation agreements, and proposed Physical AI work.','Write a conditional release recommendation stating what evidence covers and what remains unresolved.'],
'Review image delivery, activity-state availability, synchronization and authentication ownership. At the applicable levels, verify transaction adaptation before transfer and snoop-filter capacity for combined demand in the new composition. Define deployment-specific timing, fault reporting/containment/recovery, supervision and safety requirements rather than treating the proposed Physical AI workstream as a released certification. Recommend release only within the scope justified by available evidence; name the missing artifacts and decisions.',[131,146,151,157,158,165,168])]
parts=[]
for n,title,mins,case,qs,answer,pages in ex:
 links=' '.join(f'<a href="{spec}#page={p}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PDF p. {p}</a>' for p in pages)
 parts.append(f'<section id="exercise-{n}"><p class="eyebrow">Module {n} · {mins} minutes</p><h2>{n}. {title}</h2><p>{html.escape(case)}</p><ol>'+''.join('<li>'+html.escape(q)+'</li>' for q in qs)+f'</ol><label for="response-{n}">Your reasoning</label><textarea id="response-{n}" rows="7" placeholder="Record your reasoning; use Print to save a copy."></textarea><details><summary>Compare with a worked response</summary><p>{html.escape(answer)}</p></details><p class="sources">{links}</p></section>')
style='''*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;color:#444;background:#f6f8f5;line-height:1.65}main{max-width:950px;margin:auto;padding:48px 28px}h1{font-size:2.3rem;line-height:1.15;color:#343895}h2{color:#4e682b}section{background:white;border-left:5px solid #8dc63f;padding:28px;margin:28px 0;scroll-margin-top:20px}.eyebrow{font-weight:700;color:#5f6062}nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:20px}a{color:#343895}.sources{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px;font-size:.9rem}textarea{width:100%;font:inherit;padding:14px;border:1px solid #9ca3af;border-radius:6px}label{display:block;font-weight:700}details{margin-top:20px}summary{cursor:pointer;color:#343895;font-weight:700}button{padding:10px 20px;background:#343895;color:white;border:0;border-radius:6px;font:inherit;cursor:pointer}@media print{body{background:white}main{padding:0}section{break-inside:avoid;border:1px solid #bbb}details{display:block}details>*{display:block}button{display:none}}'''
(R/'resources/integration-workbook.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>FCSA integration workbook</title><style>'+style+'</style><main><p class="eyebrow">OCP Academy · FCSA</p><h1>Integration review workbook</h1><p>Five core exercises connect the course to a practical review. Allow about 36 minutes. Record your reasoning, then compare it with each worked response. These examples are teaching aids; expand them against the applicable specification and product requirements.</p><nav>'+''.join(f'<a href="#exercise-{i}">Exercise {i}</a>' for i in range(1,6))+'</nav><p><button id="print-workbook">Print or save your work</button></p>'+''.join(parts)+'<footer>FCSA: Architecting Interoperable Chiplet Systems · Specification 1.1.0. No exercise result certifies a design. Your typed responses remain on this open page and are included when printed; they are not sent to an LMS.</footer></main><script>document.getElementById(\"print-workbook\").addEventListener(\"click\",function(){window.print()});</script></html>')
